Contents of this part.
- Tool exposure: getActiveToolNames, getCallableToolNames, _getToolExposure (direct, model-only, codemode, deferred, hidden), and allowed/excluded tool filters.
- _applyToolLoadout runs prepareLoadout hooks.
- _rebuildSystemPrompt and _preparePromptAndToolLoadout use buildSystemPrompt and section diffs. Prompt snippets and guidelines are normalized.
- Hidden-declarations and forced-system-prompt projections run in transformContext.
- prompt():
  - rejects while compacting or streaming according to the streamingBehavior option;
  - input handlers may consume the input;
  - extension slash-command dispatch;
  - skill command expansion: `<skill name=".." location="..">\nReferences are relative to <baseDir>.\n\n<body>\n</skill>` with the frontmatter stripped;
  - prompt template expansion;
  - images processed through processImage under the model's input limits;
  - before_agent_start;
  - pending bash and custom messages flushed;
  - _runAgentPrompt and _handlePostAgentRun (retry, overflow recovery, before_settle).
- Queues:
  - steer/followUp follow the one-at-a-time vs all modes.
  - Extension-origin queued slash commands are treated as raw text (#2023).
  - clearQueue, pendingMessageCount.
- setModel/cycleModel: persist to the session, optionally persist defaults, per-model thinking overrides, scoped model order.
- setThinkingLevel/cycleThinkingLevel: clamp via clampThinkingLevel/getSupportedThinkingLevels.
- Steering and follow-up modes are synced from settings.

The concurrency guard is tested by agent-session-concurrent: a second prompt() while streaming throws unless streamingBehavior is set. waitForIdle and abort semantics need careful async ordering (promise resolvers become oneshot channels or condvars).

hazards:
JSON byte compatibility. JSONL entries and settings/auth/models-store files must match JSON.stringify output:
- key insertion order per object literal;
- undefined fields dropped;
- 2-space indent for config files;
- JS number formatting (shortest round-trip, exponent rules such as 1e21, -0 printed as 0);
- provider message bodies preserved verbatim.
Use serde_json with preserve_order plus a custom number formatter in Rust, and an ordered map or RawMessage in Go. Spread semantics ({...a, x}) keep an existing key's position; delete followed by re-insert moves the key to the end.

Async ordering. Many promise-chain queues must keep FIFO ordering under cancellation:
- the settings write queue;
- the InMemoryAuthStorageBackend chain;
- the NestedToolCallRunner queueTail;
- per-provider credential queues in ModelRuntime;
- coalesced reloads with reader counting.
raceWithAbortSignal rejects on abort while the underlying operation keeps running. Map AbortSignal to CancellationToken / context.Context.

reports.summary:
This area covers the extension host and the resource discovery layer in packages/coding-agent/src/core. It includes:
- the extension contract (extensions/types.ts), event dispatcher (extensions/runner.ts) and loader/runtime (extensions/loader.ts);
- resource discovery: resource-loader.ts, package-manager.ts, skills.ts, prompt-templates.ts, pi-manifest.ts, source-info.ts, slash-commands.ts;
- project trust: trust-manager.ts, project-trust.ts;
- MCP server config validation and registry (mcp-servers.ts);
- app keybindings, git branch data for the footer, HTTP dispatcher config, bug-report generation and upload, and the crash log.

Extension contract to native trait/interface. types.ts defines about 40 event payloads, each with its own result type. It also defines ExtensionAPI (on, registerTool/Command/Shortcut/Flag, renderers, markdown transformers, sendMessage/sendUserMessage/appendEntry, setActiveTools/setModel, registerProvider, registerVirtualModel, registerMcpServer, an events bus, exec), ExtensionContext and ExtensionCommandContext (UI, session, model, settings accessors), and ToolDefinition with exposure modes direct, model-only, codemode, deferred and hidden.

Native mapping:
- Rust: `trait Extension { fn name(&self) -> &str; fn register(&self, api: &mut ExtensionApi) -> Result<()> }`. ExtensionApi stores typed handler closures per event, e.g. `api.on_tool_call(|ev, ctx| async move {...})`. A closed `ExtensionEvent` enum and per-event result types back this.
- Go: `type Extension interface { Name() string; Register(api *ExtensionAPI) error }` with typed registration methods such as `OnToolCall(func(ctx *ExtensionContext, ev *ToolCallEvent) (*ToolCallEventResult, error))`. No reflection.
- ToolDefinition becomes a trait/interface: execute plus optional renderCall/renderResult hooks that take TUI types.
- ExtensionContext is a struct holding a shared handle to runner state plus a generation counter. Every accessor runs the assertActive stale check and returns the exact stale-context error text.

Runner semantics to keep exactly:
- The handler list is snapshotted per dispatch, and handlers run in extension load order.
- Most handler errors are caught and reported through onError with the event name. tool_call errors propagate. user_bash fails closed.
- session_before_* events stop at the first cancel.
- The context event deep-clones messages, then runs the context phase and then the context_with_system phase.
- The input event chains transforms and stops on "handled". before_agent_start chains system-prompt edits.
- message_end replacements must keep the original role. A tool_result content replacement drops structuredContent unless the handler supplies one.
- cache_warming_decision: the last override wins. project_trust: the first yes/no wins.
- Boundary events (turn_end, agent_before_settle) rebuild the context after each handler.
- ui_prompt_start/end are emitted on a microtask around UI prompts, with a depth counter.
- Shortcut conflicts produce diagnostics against reserved built-in keybindings. Duplicate command names get a `:N` suffix. For tools, the first registration wins.

Loader. Dropped: jiti, virtual modules, aliases, the module cache and Node SEA detection. Kept:
- createExtensionRuntime: action stubs throw until bindCore, and provider, virtual-model and MCP registrations are queued and then flushed with errors reported.
- createExtensionAPI: validations with exact messages, and commit/discard (a failed factory leaves no registrations or flag values).
- loadExtensionFromFactory, `<inline:N>` / `<inline:name>` naming, and `builtin:<name>` resolution from a native registry of mcp, codemode, llama and tool-search with the `replaceable` flag.
- Discovery: resolveExtensionEntries (package.json `pi.extensions`, then index.ts, then index.js) and discoverExtensionsInDir (one level deep).

Discovered file paths (project .pi/extensions, agentDir/extensions, settings, package `pi.extensions`) are still computed so that diagnostics and config listings match. Loading them yields a per-path load error ("Failed to load extension: <path>: ...not supported") instead of running JS.

Resource loading. DefaultResourceLoader.reload runs these steps in order:
1. optional project-trust pre-pass;
2. packageManager.resolve plus CLI temporary sources;
3. extension load with built-in replacement;
4. tool/flag conflict diagnostics;
5. skills, prompts and themes with collision dedupe;
6. AGENTS/CLAUDE context files (global first, then ancestors from root to cwd, with nested-worktree/submodule shadow detection via findGitPaths);
7. SYSTEM.md / APPEND_SYSTEM.md (project files only when trusted).

Output order depends on resourcePrecedenceRank, insertion order and canonicalized paths.

Package manager:
- npm:/git/local sources across user, project and temporary scopes;
- pattern filters (plain, !, +, -) using minimatch and node-ignore semantics, plus auto-discovery conventions;
- install/remove/update through npm/pnpm/bun and git subprocesses;
- sha256-hashed temp dirs, PI_OFFLINE handling and a 10s network timeout.

It must be ported behind an injectable command runner, because its tests spy on runCommand, runCommandCapture and spawn.

Trust and MCP:
- trust.json uses sorted keys and is guarded by a lock compatible with proper-lockfile.
- resolveProjectTrusted has a fixed resolution order: override, no trust-requiring resources, extension event, store, setting, no UI, then prompt.
- MCP config validation error strings are user-visible.

Remaining modules:
- Keybindings: app.* ids, platform-dependent defaults, legacy-name migration; extends the TUI KeybindingsManager.
- FooterDataProvider: watches git HEAD/reftable with fs watchers plus WSL polling and a 500ms debounce.
- http-dispatcher: parses and formats the idle timeout and applies proxy env vars. Natively this configures the shared reqwest client / net/http Transport.
- Bug report: redaction, archive contents and an LLM summary.
- Crash log: crashes.json with retention rules.

Suggested port order:
1. ext-types (with source-info and slash-commands);
2. keybindings/footer/http;
3. skills/prompts;
4. package-manager;
5. ext-runner;
6. loader/resource-loader;
7. trust/mcp/bug-report/crash-log.

source-info.ts imports the PathMetadata type from package-manager.ts. Natively, define PathMetadata in the source_info module to break this cycle.

hazards:
JSON byte formats:
- trust.json and crashes.json: 2-space indent plus a trailing newline;
- npm root package.json: 2-space indent, no trailing newline;
- trust.json keys are sorted.
serde_json/encoding/json defaults differ (Go escapes <>&; serde preserves order only with preserve_order).

Regex parity:
- SENSITIVE_KEY is case-insensitive and applied after a camelCase split;
- the substituteArgs alternation must be one global pass;
- the npm spec regex;
- JS /\s/ is Unicode whitespace including U+FEFF;
- crash-log /u regexes;
- decodeURI in stack-path parsing.
Go RE2 and Rust regex lack lookbehind; none is used, but check every pattern.

reports.summary:
Scope: packages/coding-agent/src/modes/interactive/ (excluding components/), which is 10,517 lines in total. It breaks down as:
- interactive-mode.ts: 7,025 lines.
- theme/: 2,215 lines of TS plus 517 lines of JSON.
- Seven small modules: 760 lines.
- assets/clankolas.png.

Architecture. InteractiveMode is the TUI front end over AgentSessionRuntime. main.ts builds it as new InteractiveMode(runtime, options) and calls run().
- The constructor wires these pieces:
  - capability overrides;
  - a renderer, either TuiMainScreen or TuiAltScreen (fullscreen), created by tui-renderer.ts;
  - a stable `ui` reference. In TS this is a Proxy that always forwards to the current renderer, so a /settings switch to fullscreen does not leave stale bindings (regression #7731);
  - chat containers;
  - KeybindingsManager;
  - CustomEditor (an editor that embeds the working-status spinner);
  - the footer;
  - InteractiveThemeController.
- init() then does the following:
  - registers SIGTERM/SIGHUP/uncaught handlers;
  - builds the header and the chat viewport (ScrollView + VStack dock: pending messages, status, widgets, editor, footer);
  - starts the TUI;
  - queries terminal colors (100 ms timeout, late replies applied) to resolve the "system" or auto light/dark theme;
  - ensures fd/rg are available;
  - binds keybindings and loads the remaining highlight languages in the background.
- run() does the following:
  - starts a bounded model-catalog refresh (15 s);
  - runs version and package-update checks, the tmux extended-keys check, startup diagnostics and the crash notice;
  - renders the initial messages;
  - loops forever on getUserInput().
- Editor submit goes through a slash-command dispatcher (/model, /settings, /tree, /login, /share, /bug, /session, /hotkeys, /compact, and so on).
  - `!` and `!!` run user bash, which fails closed through extension user_bash.
  - Otherwise the input goes to session.prompt, or to steer/followUp while streaming. During compaction it is queued.
- subscribeToAgent and handleEvent turn AgentSessionEvent variants into transcript components: assistant messages, tool executions, bash output, compaction and branch summaries, and cache-miss, cost and thinking-drop notices.
- Extensions talk to the UI through createExtensionUIContext. That surface covers:
  - select/confirm/input/editor dialogs with timeout and abort;
  - notify, status, widgets, header and footer factories;
  - custom overlays;
  - custom editor components;
  - autocomplete provider wrappers;
  - terminal input listeners;
  - working indicators.
- Selectors (settings, model, scoped models, thinking, tree, session/resume, user-message fork, trust) temporarily replace the editor through showSelector.
- Auth flows cover OAuth, API key, ambient (amazon-bedrock), llama.cpp and Radius login. They are driven by AuthEvent/AuthPrompt streams and offer to add the Radius MCP server.
- Shutdown order differs for signals and for interactive quit (see 5080/5724). Ctrl+Z suspends with SIGTSTP to the process group and restores on SIGCONT.

Theme subsystem. theme.ts holds a process-global Theme. In TS it is a globalThis Symbol singleton exposed through a Proxy.
- A Theme precomputes fg/bg ANSI per token:
  - "" means the terminal default;
  - dim tokens get special sequences;
  - bold, italic and the other modifiers come from chalk.
- Themes are JSON files with vars, var refs (circular detection) and color literals (hex, 0-255, oklch(), okhsl(), "").
- Built-in dark and light themes are okhsl JSON. Custom themes live in ~/.pi/agent/themes and are watched with fs.watch (100 ms debounce). Extensions can also register themes.
- The "system" theme (system-theme.ts) is generated from the reported terminal fg/bg/palette:
  - It uses OKHSL/OKLab contrast curves (degree-5 polynomials), family/slot rules in topological order and 20-step bisection relaxation against WCAG 4.5.
  - When no background is reported it falls back to indexed palette colors.
- theme-controller.ts handles live terminal color queries, mode 2031 scheme notifications, auto light/dark pairs, preview and rebinding after a TUI switch.
- theme-json.ts is the typebox validator. main.ts installs it lazily.

Proposed native layout. InteractiveMode stays one struct. Rust uses `impl` blocks spread across submodules: modes/interactive/interactive_mode/{helpers, core, resources, extension_ui, input, events, transcript, lifecycle, queues, selectors, auth, commands}.rs. Go uses methods spread across files in package `interactive`.
- Theme maps to modes::interactive::theme in Rust and package interactive/theme in Go.
- The Go package layering must avoid import cycles. components imports external-editor, model-search and model-catalog-refresh. So these must be at or below components: put them in a package such as interactive/support. Packages go from theme to support, then components, then interactive (InteractiveMode, tui_renderer, chat_viewport, session_share, bug_report).
- The theme is built very early, because nearly every renderer, tool, extension UI and component imports it.

Testing strategy. Most InteractiveMode tests call `Reflect.get(InteractiveMode.prototype, name).call(fakeThis, ...)` on partial fake objects. Native ports cannot do that. Port the assertions against a real InteractiveMode built over:
- the faux provider plus test/suite/harness equivalents;
- a VirtualTerminal;
- injectable seams: a CommandRunner (child processes), Clipboard, SignalSource/ProcessControl, Clock/timers, an HTTP client, and the Settings/Session traits.

Theme tests and the small-module tests port directly. Regression tests keep their issue-number comments.

External processes and network:
- spawn of $VISUAL/$EDITOR (shell: true on win32);
- gh auth status and gh gist create;
- tmux show -gv extended-keys;
- fetch to the Radius artifacts endpoint, pi.dev report-install and version checks;
- Radius bug-report upload.
All of these must use core/http-dispatcher proxy settings and honor PI_OFFLINE.

Byte compatibility applies to:
- theme JSON (camelCase color keys, the export section, the $schema URL);
- the pi.share custom session entry (8-char id from randomUUID) appended to JSONL exports;
- bug-report custom entries;
- mcp.json edits;
- debug log output;
- every ANSI sequence the theme emits.

hazards:
Byte-exact ANSI output:
- fg/bg per token;
- "" emits default 39/49;
- dim fg `\x1b[2m...\x1b[22;39m`;
- chalk modifier nesting and newline encasing;
- truecolor vs 256 selection via getTerminalColorMode.
Rendering tests compare raw or stripped output.

Timing constants:
- 100ms terminal color query timeout with late replies;
- 100ms theme watcher debounce;
- 500ms double-Escape and double-Ctrl+C windows;
- 15s catalog refresh bound;
- 2s tmux check;
- 5s telemetry fetch;
- 1000ms drainInput on shutdown.
Tests use fake timers, so expose an injectable clock.

On-disk and wire formats:
- pi.share custom entry (key order, 8-char id, ISO timestamp) appended to JSONL;
- bug-report custom entry;
- mcp.json edits for the Radius MCP offer;
- debug log JSON-escaped lines;
- Radius artifact POST headers and query string.
JSON serializers must not HTML-escape (Go json.Encoder SetEscapeHTML(false)) and must preserve insertion order (serde_json preserve_order or struct field order).

Unicode and regex details:
- /\p{Cc}/u control-character check on pasted file paths;
- /\b(?:abort(?:ed)?|cancel(?:l?ed)?)\b/i abort detection;
- /[^a-zA-Z0-9_\-./~:@]/ quoting in formatResumeCommand;
- /\/index\.(ts|js)$/ stripping;
- /^ok(lch|hsl)\(/i color literal detection.
Rust regex has no lookaround; none is needed here.

